  {
   "id": "_-I2BAsKFwAJ",
   "from": "Guy Ashiri-Prossner",
   "author": "6e686d5caf49848a",
   "date": "2018-09-23T12:38:41Z",
   "subject": "Re: LISREL summaries",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">A toy example is nice, but I try to understand what&#39;s behind.  Vandenberg defines it as &quot;\r\n<span>An omnibus test of the equality of covariance matrices across groups</span>&quot;, there ought to be a named method behind this, a comparison method which yields a chi-square statistic - does it mean Box’s M-test? something else?<br style=\"font-variant-numeric:normal;font-variant-east-asian:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:-webkit-auto\"></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "MtOvB-JVGQAJ",
   "from": "Rönkkö, Mikko",
   "author": "bfa43814f6ba1969",
   "date": "2018-09-23T12:46:28Z",
   "subject": "Re: LISREL summaries",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\">\r\nHi,\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>The test is explained in the cited paper, but not as clearly as it could be. It is basically a multi group SEM, where you fit a saturated model where are variance and covariances are fee, but constrain these to be the same across groups.</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>The test is similar to Box’s M, but not equivalent because it is based on maximum likelihood estimates instead of calculated directly from the covariances.</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>Mikko<br>\r\n<div><br>\r\n<blockquote type=\"cite\"></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\">\r\n<div>On 23 Sep 2018, at 15.38, Guy Ashiri-Prossner &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">guy.a...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:</div>\r\n<br>\r\n</blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div>\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\">A toy example is nice, but I try to understand what&#39;s behind.  Vandenberg defines it as &quot;\r\n<span>An omnibus test of the equality of covariance matrices across groups</span>&quot;, there ought to be a named method behind this, a comparison method which yields a chi-square statistic - does it mean Box’s M-test? something else?<br style=\"font-variant-numeric:normal;font-variant-east-asian:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:-webkit-auto\">\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
